WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.642 --> 00:00:18.764
night there was little change in our situation; we were still under five hundred feet of ice.

2
00:00:19.725 --> 00:00:24.608
During the night I woke up several times and found that we were slowly rising to the surface

3
00:00:24.708 --> 00:00:26.749
as the layer of ice above us grew thinner.

4
00:00:27.809 --> 00:00:32.252
Finally, at six o'clock in the morning, Captain Nemo came to the door of the gallery.

5
00:00:33.312 --> 00:00:38.356
"The sea is open," was all he said before quickly departing, but that was enough for me.

6
00:00:39.417 --> 00:00:43.721
I went up to the platform, where the captain was looking at the water; it was still crowded

7
00:00:43.761 --> 00:00:45.843
with pieces of ice and moving icebergs.

8
00:00:45.863 --> 00:00:47.944
"Are we at the South Pole?"

9
00:00:48.225 --> 00:00:48.625
I asked.

10
00:00:49.646 --> 00:00:51.147
"I don't know," he replied.

11
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:54.630
"I'll take our bearings at noon to determine our location."

12
00:00:55.551 --> 00:01:00.455
Captain Nemo invited my companions and me to join him when he set foot on land, but only

13
00:01:00.475 --> 00:01:01.776
Conseil and I were interested.

14
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.622
Two crew members rowed us to shore in the small boat.

15
00:01:06.302 --> 00:01:08.624
Conseil was about to jump out when I held him back.

16
00:01:09.745 --> 00:01:15.188
"Sir," I said to Captain Nemo, "the honor of being first to set foot on land belongs to

17
00:01:15.248 --> 00:01:17.990
you." "Thank you," said the captain.

18
00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:23.714
He climbed out of the boat, and after a few minutes said to us, "Whenever you like We

19
00:01:23.754 --> 00:01:27.477
followed him onto the rocky shore, where we observed seals and penguins.

20
00:01:28.617 --> 00:01:33.725
Unfortunately, it was too foggy to see the sun and take our bearings, so we were still not

21
00:01:33.845 --> 00:01:35.226
sure about where we had landed.

22
00:01:36.286 --> 00:01:40.429
We returned to the submarine, disappointed but hoping to have better luck the next day.

23
00:01:41.489 --> 00:01:47.433
Unfortunately the following morning, our view of the sun was once again obscured, this time by heavy snow.

24
00:01:48.914 --> 00:01:54.257
Finally, on the third day, Captain Nemo led us toward a peak, where we watched the sun burst

25
00:01:54.317 --> 00:01:55.037
through the clouds.

26
00:01:56.098 --> 00:01:59.400
He made his observations and confirmed that we were at the South Pole.

27
00:02:00.696 --> 00:02:09.899
"I, Captain Nemo, have reached the South Pole today, March 21, 1868, and I take possession of this

28
00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:11.859
part of the globe," he said gravely.

29
00:02:11.879 --> 00:02:14.220
"In the name of which monarch?"

30
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:19.081
I asked, remembering how an explorer usually claimed land on behalf of his ruler.

31
00:02:19.101 --> 00:02:20.882
"In my own name!"

32
00:02:21.322 --> 00:02:25.823
replied Captain Nemo, holding up a black flag bearing a gold N.

33
00:02:27.043 --> 00:02:31.806
The next morning, at six o'clock, the crew of the Nautilus prepared to leave the South Pole.

34
00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:36.928
With its reservoirs filling with water, the submarine began its slow descent.

35
00:02:38.449 --> 00:02:43.732
At one thousand feet below the surface, it headed north at a steady 15 miles per hour.

36
00:02:44.793 --> 00:02:48.035
Toward night it was already floating under an immense iceberg.

37
00:02:49.075 --> 00:02:52.297
At three o'clock in the morning, I was awakened by a violent shock.

38
00:02:53.358 --> 00:02:57.160
I sat up in bed and was thrown into the middle of the room by another shock.

39
00:02:58.208 --> 00:03:02.129
The Nautilus having struck something, had rebounded and hit something else.

40
00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:06.230
I rushed to the gallery, where I found the furniture overturned.

41
00:03:07.311 --> 00:03:11.312
I heard footsteps and excited voices, but Captain Nemo did not appear.

42
00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:15.153
As I was leaving the gallery, Ned and Conseil entered.

43
00:03:15.173 --> 00:03:16.853
"What's going on?"

44
00:03:17.394 --> 00:03:17.914
I asked them.

45
00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:21.975
"That's what I came to ask you, Master," said Conseil.

46
00:03:23.015 --> 00:03:23.795
Ned scowled.

47
00:03:24.136 --> 00:03:25.536
"The Nautilus has run aground.

48
00:03:26.892 --> 00:03:31.555
Judging from the way it's lying, I doubt it'll right itself as it did in the Torres Strait."

49
00:03:32.835 --> 00:03:34.736
"So, do you think we're at the surface?"

50
00:03:35.257 --> 00:03:35.877
asked Conseil.

51
00:03:35.897 --> 00:03:38.118
"That's easy enough to find out."

52
00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:43.881
I consulted the instruments, and to my great surprise, I discovered that we were more than a thousand

53
00:03:44.021 --> 00:03:44.902
feet underwater.

54
00:03:45.922 --> 00:03:47.363
"We must speak to Captain Nemo."

55
00:03:48.324 --> 00:03:52.386
The three of us went in search of the captain, but when we couldn't find him, I decided

56
00:03:52.426 --> 00:03:53.606
he must be in the pilothouse.

57
00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.348
We returned to the gallery to wait for him.

58
00:03:57.468 --> 00:04:01.632
After about twenty minutes, the captain entered the room, but he took no notice of us.

59
00:04:02.773 --> 00:04:05.655
His normally emotionless face showed signs of unease.

60
00:04:06.756 --> 00:04:10.019
He watched the compass silently and then read the other instruments.

61
00:04:11.119 --> 00:04:15.163
I didn't want to interrupt him, but when he turned toward me, I decided to speak.

62
00:04:15.183 --> 00:04:17.925
"An incident, Captain?"

63
00:04:18.345 --> 00:04:21.808
I asked, using the phrase he'd preferred in the Torres Strait.

64
00:04:22.869 --> 00:04:24.590
"No, Professor, it's an accident."

65
00:04:25.691 --> 00:04:28.560
"Serious?"' "Perhaps," he replied.

66
00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.522
"We've run aground underwater, but we aren't in immediate danger."

67
00:04:32.542 --> 00:04:36.243
"May I ask, sir, what caused this accident?"

68
00:04:37.203 --> 00:04:43.865
"An enormous block of ice, an entire iceberg, turned over and slid under the Nautilus Captain Nemo said

69
00:04:43.905 --> 00:04:48.646
that the submarine was now emptying its reservoirs in an attempt to right itself, but the block of

70
00:04:48.786 --> 00:04:49.787
ice was rising with it.

71
00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:53.748
He explained this while never taking his eyes from the instruments.

72
00:04:54.812 --> 00:05:00.457
Suddenly we felt a small movement below us; evidently the submarine was righting itself slightly.

73
00:05:01.938 --> 00:05:07.823
Ten long minutes passed while we watched the pictures on the wall slowly return to their normal position

74
00:05:08.304 --> 00:05:10.586
and the floor become horizontal under our feet.

75
00:05:10.606 --> 00:05:12.627
"Are we floating again?"

76
00:05:13.068 --> 00:05:13.468
I asked.

77
00:05:13.488 --> 00:05:18.928
"Of course," replied the captain, "but we're still underwater."

78
00:05:18.948 --> 00:05:23.790
I looked through the windows and saw that a dazzling wall of ice rose about ten yards away

79
00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:28.792
from either side of the Nautilus There was also ice above and below us.

80
00:05:29.813 --> 00:05:33.654
We were imprisoned in a perfect tunnel of ice filled with water.

81
00:05:34.615 --> 00:05:36.936
But I decided it would be easy to escape the tunnel.

82
00:05:37.896 --> 00:05:42.178
The submarine could go either forward or backward and then dive under the iceberg.

83
00:05:43.158 --> 00:05:47.740
Captain Nemo left us, and I assumed he returned to the pilothouse.

84
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.962
My companions and I stayed at the gallery windows, dazzled by the submarine's light sparkling on the ice.

85
00:05:54.503 --> 00:05:59.625
At five in the morning, we felt a shock at the vessel's bow; its battering ram had obviously

86
00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:00.706
struck a block of ice.

87
00:06:00.726 --> 00:06:06.708
"We must go in reverse now, and see if we can find another way out," I said, trying

88
00:06:06.748 --> 00:06:07.588
to appear cheerful.

89
00:06:07.608 --> 00:06:11.050
"That'll take hours more," grumbled Ned.

90
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.511
"How long are we going to be stuck here?"

91
00:06:14.712 --> 00:06:18.121
I had no idea, so I went to the library to calm my nerves.

92
00:06:19.201 --> 00:06:21.342
I threw myself on a couch and tried to read a book.

93
00:06:22.363 --> 00:06:25.324
Fifteen minutes later Conseil and Ned entered the room.

94
00:06:25.344 --> 00:06:28.045
"Is that an interesting book, Master?"

95
00:06:28.645 --> 00:06:29.266
asked Conseil.

96
00:06:29.286 --> 00:06:31.767
"Very interesting," I replied.

97
00:06:31.787 --> 00:06:33.367
"Well, it should be."

98
00:06:33.787 --> 00:06:34.428
Conseil laughed.

99
00:06:35.248 --> 00:06:36.269
"It's the one you wrote!"

100
00:06:37.349 --> 00:06:39.930
I slammed the book shut, and the two of them turned to go.

101
00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:41.211
I stood up.

102
00:06:41.231 --> 00:06:44.192
"Let's return to the gallery," I said.

103
00:06:44.908 --> 00:06:47.149
"We should stay together until we're out of this predicament."

104
00:06:48.049 --> 00:06:50.190
"As you wish, Master," replied Conseil.

105
00:06:51.231 --> 00:06:55.873
Several hours passed while the Nautilus moved backward at a good rate of speed, and we kept an

106
00:06:55.993 --> 00:06:56.753
eye on the instruments.

107
00:06:57.834 --> 00:07:01.976
It was almost 8:30 when we felt another shock, this time at the stern.

108
00:07:03.036 --> 00:07:05.637
I turned pale and seized Conseil's hand.

109
00:07:06.658 --> 00:07:08.659
The three of us exchanged troubled glances.

110
00:07:09.719 --> 00:07:11.660
At this point Captain Nemo entered the room.

111
00:07:12.748 --> 00:07:16.714
"The iceberg has shifted and closed up every outlet," he announced.

112
00:07:17.835 --> 00:07:19.938
Once again we were trapped in the ice!
